FEATURES ------------------------------------------------------------


Use chroot only if supported[DONE rsync 3.2.0, 2020/06/19]

  rsync now fully supports chroot on platforms where chroot(2) is
  available.  As of rsync 3.2.0, the "use chroot" parameter in
  rsyncd.conf defaults to "unset", which means rsync will use chroot
  automatically when it can and fall back to a sanitized path traversal
  when it cannot (e.g. when not running as root).  Prior to that, the
  fallback behaviour was introduced incrementally across the 3.x series.

                      --          --


Allow supplementary groups in rsyncd.conf.5.md  Perhaps allow supplementary groups to be specified in rsyncd.conf;
  then make the first one the primary gid and all the rest be
  supplementary gids.

                      --          --


Handling IPv6 on old machines[DONE rsync 2.5.0, 2001/11/30]

  IPv6 support was added in rsync 2.5.0 based on a patch from KAME.net.
  rsync was rewritten to use the modern getaddrinfo()/getnameinfo()
  interface.  Compatibility fallback implementations are provided in
  lib/ (getaddrinfo.c, inet_ntop.c, inet_pton.c) for systems that
  lack those functions.  The KAME-era lib/getaddrinfo.c compatibility
  code was substantially revised in rsync 3.0.7 and later.

                      --          --


Other IPv6 stuff[DONE rsync 2.5.0+, 2001/11/30]

  Multi-address connect (trying each address returned by getaddrinfo()
  in turn until one succeeds) is implemented in socket.c.  Listening on
  multiple passive addresses for daemons is also handled.  Literal IPv6
  addresses in HOST:PATH syntax (e.g. [::1]) were added in rsync 2.6.3.
  IPv6 literals in rsync: URLs and in rsyncd.conf hosts allow/deny
  rules were added in subsequent releases.

                      --          --


Add ACL support/[DONE rsync 3.0.0, 2008/03/01]

  ACL transfer was fully implemented in rsync 3.0.0 via the --acls (-A)
  option.  Both POSIX ACLs and OS X ACLs are supported.  The
  implementation lives in acls.c and integrates with the uidlist.c
  name-mapping code so that ACL user/group names are translated across
  systems.  A --fake-super mode also allows non-root users to store
  foreign ACL data via extended attributes.

                      --          --


proxy authentication[DONE rsync 2.5.x]

  HTTP Basic Proxy-Authentication is fully implemented.  Set
  RSYNC_PROXY to user:pass@proxy.foo:3128/ and rsync will parse
  out the user and password and send a Base64-encoded
  Proxy-Authorization: Basic header in the HTTP CONNECT request.  The
  code lives in socket.c (establish_proxy_connection()).

                      --          --


SOCKS

  Add --with-socks, and then perhaps a command-line option to put them
  on or off.  This might be more reliable than LD_PRELOAD hacks.

                      --          --


FAT support

  rsync to a FAT partition on a Unix machine doesn't work very well at
  the moment.  I think we get errors about invalid filenames and
  perhaps also trying to do atomic renames.

  I guess the code to do this is currently #ifdef'd on Windows;
  perhaps we ought to intelligently fall back to it on Unix too.

                      --          --


--diffdavid.e.sewell2002/03/15

  Allow people to specify the diff command.  (Might want to use wdiff,
  gnudiff, etc.)

  Just diff the temporary file with the destination file, and delete
  the tmp file rather than moving it into place.

  Interaction with --partial.

  Security interactions with daemon mode?

                      --          --


Add daemon --no-fork options.c E rsync 2.5.1, 2002/01/03]

  Implemented as --no-detach in rsync 2.5.1.  Required to run rsync as
  a Windows service, and also useful when running under daemontools,
  AIX SRC, or a debugger.  The option prevents the daemon from
  forking into the background and dissociating from the terminal,
  making it possible to see log output on stderr and to attach a
  debugger directly.

                      --          --


Create more granular verbosity2003/05/15

  Control output with the --report option.

  The option takes as a single argument (no whitespace) a
  comma delimited lists of keywords.

  This would separate debugging from "logging" as well as
  fine grained selection of statistical reporting and what
  actions are logged.

  https://lists.samba.org/archive/rsync/2003-May/006059.html

                      --          --

DOCUMENTATION --------------------------------------------------------


Keep list of open issues and todos on the web site

                      --          --


Perhaps redo manual as SGML[DONE rsync 3.2.0, 2020/06/19]

  Done, but as Markdown rather than SGML.  In rsync 3.2.0 the manpages
  were converted from yodl to Markdown (.md files), processed via a
  Python 3 script using the cmarkgfm or commonmark library.  The
  resulting sources are rsync.1.md, rsyncd.conf.5.md, and
  rsync-ssl.1.md.  This made it much easier to package rsync since yodl
  was a relatively obscure dependency.

                      --          --

LOGGING --------------------------------------------------------------


Memory accounting

  At exit, show how much memory was used for the file list, etc.

  We also do a weird exponential-growth allocation in flist.c.  I'm
  not sure this makes sense with modern mallocs.  At any rate it will
  make us allocate a huge amount of memory for large file lists.

                      --          --


Improve error messages

  If we hang or get SIGINT, then explain where we were up to.  Perhaps
  have a static buffer that contains the current function name, or
  some kind of description of what we were trying to do.  This is a
  little easier on people than needing to run strace/truss.

  "The dungeon collapses!  You are killed."  Rather than "unexpected
  eof" give a message that is more detailed if possible and also more
  helpful.

  If we get an error writing to a socket, then we should perhaps
  continue trying to read to see if an error message comes across
  explaining why the socket is closed.  I'm not sure if this would
  work, but it would certainly make our messages more helpful.

  What happens if a directory is missing -x attributes.  Do we lose
  our load?  (Debian #28416) Probably fixed now, but a test case would
  be good.

                      --          --


